    explanation: "PyTorch uses dynamic computation graphs, meaning the graph is built on the fly during the forward pass, which makes debugging and variable-length inputs easier compared to static graph frameworks. A custom model subclasses nn.Module and implements a forward method describing how input tensors flow through layers. Training loops are explicit: you zero gradients, run a forward pass, compute loss, call loss.backward() for autograd-based gradient computation, and then step the optimizer. This explicitness gives fine-grained control that's popular in research settings, while libraries like PyTorch Lightning abstract the loop for production use.",
    code: "import torch\nimport torch.nn as nn\nimport torch.optim as optim\n\nclass SimpleNet(nn.Module):\n    def __init__(self):\n        super().__init__()\n        self.fc1 = nn.Linear(20, 64)\n        self.fc2 = nn.Linear(64, 1)\n\n    def forward(self, x):\n        x = torch.relu(self.fc1(x))\n        return torch.sigmoid(self.fc2(x))\n\nmodel = SimpleNet()\noptimizer = optim.Adam(model.parameters(), lr=1e-3)\ncriterion = nn.BCELoss()\n\nX = torch.rand(32, 20)\ny = torch.randint(0, 2, (32, 1)).float()\n\nfor epoch in range(3):\n    optimizer.zero_grad()\n    outputs = model(X)\n    loss = criterion(outputs, y)\n    loss.backward()\n    optimizer.step()\n    print(f'epoch {epoch} loss {loss.item():.4f}')",
