    summary: "Tokenization converts raw text into discrete units the model can process, and subword schemes balance vocabulary size against handling unseen words.",
    explanation: "Modern LLMs typically use subword tokenization algorithms like Byte-Pair Encoding (BPE) or WordPiece, which break text into frequently occurring chunks rather than whole words or individual characters. This approach keeps the vocabulary size manageable while still being able to represent rare or unseen words by composing them from known subword pieces, avoiding the out-of-vocabulary problem that plain word-level tokenizers face. Each token maps to an integer ID that gets embedded into a vector before entering the model. Tokenization also directly affects context window usage and API costs, since pricing and limits are usually measured in tokens, not characters or words.",
    code: "from transformers import AutoTokenizer\n\ntokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained('gpt2')\n\ntext = 'Tokenization handles unfamiliarwords gracefully.'\ntokens = tokenizer.tokenize(text)\nids = tokenizer.encode(text)\n\nprint('Tokens:', tokens)\nprint('Token IDs:', ids)\nprint('Token count:', len(ids))\nprint('Decoded:', tokenizer.decode(ids))",
