    id: "pyai-rag",
    category: "pyai",
    difficulty: "Advanced",
    topic: "LLM Applications",
    title: "How does Retrieval-Augmented Generation reduce hallucination in LLM responses?",
    summary: "RAG retrieves relevant documents from an external knowledge source and injects them into the prompt so the LLM can ground its answer in real data.",
    explanation: "A RAG pipeline embeds the user query, retrieves the most similar chunks from a vector store, and constructs a prompt that includes those chunks as context alongside the original question. This lets the LLM answer using up-to-date or proprietary information it wasn't trained on, without needing to fine-tune the model. It also improves factual grounding since the model can cite or paraphrase retrieved content rather than relying purely on parametric memory, which reduces (but doesn't eliminate) hallucination. Production RAG systems often add reranking, chunk-size tuning, and hybrid search (combining keyword and vector search) to improve retrieval quality, since the whole pipeline's accuracy is bottlenecked by retrieval relevance.",
    code: "from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()\n\ndef retrieve(query, docs, top_k=2):\n    # simplified: assume a vector_store.search exists\n    return vector_store.search(query, top_k=top_k)\n\ndef rag_answer(query):\n    context_chunks = retrieve(query, docs=None)\n    context = '\\n'.join(context_chunks)\n    prompt = f'Answer using only this context:\\n{context}\\n\\nQuestion: {query}'\n    response = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model='gpt-4o-mini',\n        messages=[{'role': 'user', 'content': prompt}],\n    )\n    return response.choices[0].message.content\n\nprint(rag_answer('What is our refund policy?'))",
    interviewQuestion: "Your RAG system returns confidently wrong answers even though the correct document is in the knowledge base. What parts of the pipeline would you investigate first?",
