    title: "What are the key considerations when deploying an ML model to production?",
    summary: "Deploying models requires wrapping inference in a stable API, managing latency and scaling, versioning models, and monitoring for drift over time.",
    explanation: "Common deployment patterns include wrapping a model in a REST or gRPC service, using batch inference for offline jobs, or embedding lightweight models directly on-device. Considerations include latency requirements, which may push toward model quantization or distillation, autoscaling to handle variable load, and containerizing with Docker for consistent environments across dev and prod. Model versioning and rollback strategies matter because model behavior can degrade due to data drift, where the distribution of production inputs diverges from training data over time. Monitoring should track both system metrics (latency, throughput, errors) and model-specific metrics (prediction distributions, confidence scores) to catch silent failures that don't throw exceptions but still produce bad predictions.",
    code: "from fastapi import FastAPI\nimport joblib\nimport numpy as np\n\napp = FastAPI()\nmodel = joblib.load('model.pkl')\n\n@app.post('/predict')\ndef predict(features: list[float]):\n    X = np.array(features).reshape(1, -1)\n    prediction = model.predict(X)[0]\n    probability = model.predict_proba(X)[0].max()\n    return {'prediction': int(prediction), 'confidence': float(probability)}\n\n# Run with: uvicorn app:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000 --workers 4",
    interviewQuestion: "How would you detect and respond to data drift for a model that's been running in production for six months?",
