    summary: "Open-source models like Llama, Mistral, and Qwen give you full control over hosting, fine-tuning, and data privacy, at the cost of managing infrastructure yourself.",
    explanation: "Open-source LLMs can be self-hosted, which keeps data on-premise or in your own cloud, important for privacy-sensitive or regulated industries, and avoids per-token API costs at high volume. However, self-hosting requires managing GPU infrastructure, optimizing inference (quantization, batching, serving frameworks like vLLM or TGI), and keeping up with model updates yourself. Proprietary APIs like those from OpenAI or Anthropic typically lead in raw capability and require zero infrastructure management, but come with per-request costs, less control over data handling, and dependency on the provider's uptime and rate limits. Many teams use a hybrid approach: open-source models for cost-sensitive or privacy-sensitive workloads, and proprietary APIs for tasks needing top-tier reasoning quality.",
    code: "from vllm import LLM, SamplingParams\n\nllm = LLM(model='mistralai/Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2', dtype='bfloat16')\n\nsampling_params = SamplingParams(temperature=0.7, max_tokens=200)\n\nprompts = ['Explain the benefits of open-source LLMs in two sentences.']\noutputs = llm.generate(prompts, sampling_params)\n\nfor output in outputs:\n    print(output.outputs[0].text)",
